WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.943
Every animal must balance water and salt gains and losses.

2
00:00:07.903 --> 00:00:09.963
The surroundings keep pulling them the other way.

3
00:00:10.763 --> 00:00:13.403
A marine bony fish loses water and gains salt.

4
00:00:14.223 --> 00:00:16.963
In fresh water, water enters and salts leak out.

5
00:00:17.703 --> 00:00:20.543
On land, evaporation threatens the water supply.

6
00:00:21.523 --> 00:00:26.463
Even marine invertebrates whose body fluids follow the sea must adjust organic

7
00:00:26.463 --> 00:00:28.063
molecules inside their cells.

8
00:00:28.843 --> 00:00:31.043
That keeps the cells from swelling or shrinking.

9
00:00:31.043 --> 00:00:34.263
Nitrogen disposal also uses water.

10
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:37.333
Marine bony fish drink seawater.

11
00:00:38.253 --> 00:00:40.253
The intestine absorbs water and ions.

12
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:43.853
Excess sodium and chloride leave mostly through the gills.

13
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:49.713
Absorbed magnesium and sulfate leave through the kidneys in a small volume of urine.

14
00:00:50.693 --> 00:00:53.133
Freshwater bony fish face the reverse problem.

15
00:00:54.013 --> 00:00:55.913
Their kidneys make plenty of dilute urine.

16
00:00:56.793 --> 00:01:00.093
Their gills actively take up sodium and chloride, using ATP.

17
00:01:01.073 --> 00:01:05.633
In salmon and eels moving between these worlds, gill cells switch between salt

18
00:01:05.633 --> 00:01:06.693
uptake and secretion.

19
00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:09.773
Protein breakdown produces ammonia.

20
00:01:10.753 --> 00:01:15.833
It needs no ATP to form, but it is toxic and takes plenty of water to excrete.

21
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:18.193
Urea costs ATP to make.

22
00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:21.193
It is less toxic and needs less water.

23
00:01:22.193 --> 00:01:25.553
Uric acid leaves as a paste or solid with very little water.

24
00:01:26.393 --> 00:01:28.333
It is usually costlier still to make.

25
00:01:29.173 --> 00:01:31.793
Each waste trades toxicity, water and energy.

26
00:01:32.817 --> 00:01:34.457
Organs control what leaves.

27
00:01:35.417 --> 00:01:40.857
Most vertebrate kidneys filter body fluid, then reabsorb useful substances and

28
00:01:40.857 --> 00:01:42.337
secrete others into the tubule.

29
00:01:43.257 --> 00:01:45.617
Insect Malpighian tubules do not filter.

30
00:01:46.417 --> 00:01:50.197
They secrete ions into the tubule, and water follows by osmosis.

31
00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:55.017
Wastes enter too. The rectum then recovers salts and water.

32
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.420
Sharks retain urea for another purpose: water balance.

33
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.760
Urea and other dissolved particles bring their blood slightly above seawater in

34
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.700
total concentration, so water enters slowly.

35
00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.660
Their kidneys recover most filtered urea.

36
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.860
They still regulate salts.

37
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.780
A rectal gland secretes concentrated sodium chloride.

38
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:21.383
One smaller example: seabirds use nasal salt glands.

39
00:02:22.563 --> 00:02:27.243
Salt secretion more concentrated than seawater lets them clear a gulp of seawater's

40
00:02:27.243 --> 00:02:29.223
salt in less water than the gulp held.

41
00:02:30.063 --> 00:02:31.023
They keep the difference.
